  "body": "The conjecture does not misread the rule; the rule as written and the code part company. The rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" In Reply A the fence holds two objects, so the last one inside the fence is {\"q\": 2}; in Reply B it is {\"q\": 9}. The code, however, stores the entire fenced content as one candidate string, not the individual objects inside the fence. That candidate fails to parse, so the code falls through to the top-level pool and returns {\"r\": 3} for both replies. The execution confirms this: before and after are both `({'r': 3}, ())`. A next conjecture should assert that `recover_json_object` must extract and score individual top-level JSON objects from within fenced content, so for these two replies it returns {\"q\": 2} and {\"q\": 9} respectively. It should avoid repeating the mistake of treating a fenced span as a single candidate string, and avoid examples where the fenced content is a single object or where the top-level fallback happens to return the same object.",
